  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to make a forecast for when one of the Millennium Prize Problems, excluding Poincar\u00e9, might be solved with the help of AI. The deadline is up to 2050, with probabilities for each year. Let's start by recalling the problems: Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture, Hodge conjecture, Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness, P versus NP, Riemann hypothesis, and Yang-Mills existence and mass gap.\n\nFirst, I should consider the current state of each problem. As of 2025, these are still open, and solving them is considered extremely challenging. The use of AI in mathematics has been growing, but I'm not sure to what extent it can contribute to proving such deep conjectures. For example, AI has been used in pattern recognition and search, but rigorous proofs might require a deeper understanding and creativity that current AI lacks, even if future AI systems are more advanced.\n\nFor each problem, I should consider the likelihood of a breakthrough, and how AI could contribute. Let's take P vs NP. A lot of progress has been made in complexity theory, but a solution might be decades away. AI could help by finding new proof techniques or identifying patterns in data, but it's unclear. Similarly, for the Riemann Hypothesis, there's a lot of numerical evidence, but a proof remains elusive. AI might help in analyzing this data or suggesting new approaches.\n\nNavier-Stokes is about the existence and smoothness of solutions. AI might be useful in simulating fluid dynamics or identifying properties that have been overlooked. The Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture and the Hodge conjecture relate to number theory and algebraic geometry, areas where abstract proofs are required. AI might assist in generating conjectures or testing them against large datasets of examples, but proving them might require more. Yang-Mills is about particles and equations in physics; here, maybe AI can help in theoretical modeling or numerical simulations.\n\nNow, considering the timeline. By 2026, which is just a year after the forecast date, it's unlikely any of these will be solved with AI. The problems are too hard and already have been open for a long time. Maybe a 0.1 chance? For 2030, four years out. Still low, but maybe slightly higher since more time for AI tools to develop. 0.1 to 0.2? By 2035, which is ten years later, maybe 0.2 to 0.3. By 2040, maybe 0.3 to 0.5. By 2050, 0.7 seems plausible, but maybe slightly higher? But I need to make sure the probabilities are non-decreasing. So starting at 0 for 2026, then maybe 0.1 for 2030, 0.15 for 2035, 0.2 for 2040, and 0.3 for 2050? But that's just me. Another angle: maybe some problems are more approachable. For example, P vs NP is considered very hard, but maybe someone close now. But even then, AI contributing a key step is uncertain. Maybe a 0.05 by 2030, 0.08 by 2035, 0.1 by 2040, and 0.2 by 2050. Hmm. But perhaps I'm too cautious. The prompt mentions \"at least one\" of the problems, so maybe the probabilities are less than for individual problems. Also, considering AI's role, even if a problem is solved, AI's contribution could be a small part. For example, if AI helps in proving a key lemma or suggesting a new approach. The Clay problems are abstract, but maybe by 2050 AI is widely used in mathematical research. I'm uncertain, but I need to be precise. Let me set 0 for 2026, 0 for 2030, maybe 0.1 for 2035, 0.2 for 2040, and 0.3 for 2050. Or maybe 2030 has a 0.02 chance. Wait, the problem says \"give one probability for at least one qualifying solution by each deadline\". So the probabilities need to be non-decreasing. So perhaps starting at 0.05 for 2026, 0.1 for 2030, 0.2 for 2035, 0.3 for 2040, and 0.5 for 2050. But the first deadline is 2026, only a year later. So probably low. Perhaps 0 for 2026, 0.05 for 2030, 0.1 for 2035, 0.2 for 2040, 0.3 for 2050. Alternatively, maybe 0 for 2026, 0.05 for 2030, then increasing each time. I think the answer here is more of a guess, but the main idea is to have a non-decreasing sequence with higher probabilities further out. So, my final answer in JSON would be:\n{\"2026\": 0, \"2030\": 0.05, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.2, \"2050\": 0.35}\nBut maybe even lower, but these numbers need to be plausible. Let me adjust. Maybe up to 0.5 in 2050. So maybe 0.4. Let me check again. The user said \"the problems that were open at the forecast date\", which in 2025. All six are still open except Poincar\u00e9. So I need to consider which ones might be more likely to be solved. For example, maybe Yang-Mills and mass gap might be approached with AI due to physics simulations. But I don't know. Anyway, given all this, here's the JSON.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0, \"2030\": 0.05, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.25, \"2050\": 0.4}",
